Carrefour-Choc

Ingredients: ZOUK + POWER METAL + COZY, WARM & CHILL LO-FI + Xylophone  
Creative Catalyst: Born when anti-austerity saboteurs spliced high-voltage guitar leads and Antillean dance sequencers into the municipal traffic-light grid of Fort-de-France and Marseille.

Carrefour-Choc is an astonishing collision of sun-drenched French-Antillean zouk syncopation, operatic neo-classical power metal guitar acrobatics, and the crackling, tape-hiss velvet of cozy bedroom lo-fi. Over warm, vinyl-baked Rhodes chords and gently looping Caribbean ti-bwa wooden clatters, lightning-fast twin electric guitars duel in soaring European power metal thirds—tempered not by stadium bombast, but by the crystalline, rounded mallet-strikes of chromatic rosewood xylophones and rain-slick street ambience. It sounds like a dragon-slaying power metal anthem muffled beneath a heavy duvet, swaying its hips on a balmy Martinican roundabout while traffic signals flicker in hypnotic, rhythmic protest.

Genre DNA

- Tempo & Pulse: 120–135 BPM. The pulse hinges on the irresistible, swing-heavy chouval bwa and zouk-love kick-snare stutter, overlaid with rolling, hyper-speed double-bass drum flutter that is mixed deceptively soft, pillowed inside downsampled tape saturation.
- Harmonic Palette: Glorious, triumphant Dorian and Mixolydian power-metal cadences softened into lush major 7th and 9th chord clusters; baroque counterpoint runs played across xylophone bars that resolve into sunny, bittersweet cadences.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Optical-Relay Sidechaining: Sub-bass and rhythm tracks duck subtly beneath real analog hiss modulated to the blinking frequencies of hacked LED pedestrian signals.
  2. Cassette-Baffled Shred: Sweeping arpeggio guitar solos are tracked through miniature 1-watt battery amps, recorded straight onto worn ferric tape, then saturated with cozy room reverb to kill harsh high-end shrieks.
  3. Mallet Resonator Tracking: The xylophone is close-miked with contact piezo pickups glued directly to the rosewood keys, catching mechanical fingernail clicks, felt thuds, and warm harmonic overtones.
- Dynamics: Songs open like sleepy, rainy-afternoon study beats—rain on asphalt, sleepy cassette flutter, solitary xylophone figures—before suddenly catching fire as sweeping, soaring twin-lead guitars bloom through the lo-fi haze, cruising at maximum melodic ecstasy without ever shattering the comforting, intimate domesticity of the room.

Scene & Origin Lore

The movement sparked during the rolling port and transit strikes across the French Caribbean and southern mainland coasts, when insurgent sound crews known as Les Relais cracked open roadside traffic controller cabinets (the ubiquitous grey metal boxes on street corners) to siphon municipal three-phase power. Splicing modified drum machines, tube preamps, and toy xylophones directly into the pneumatic loop sensors embedded under the tarmac, they turned entire four-way intersections into colossal acoustic instruments: passing city buses triggered 808 sub-kicks, while changing red-and-amber beacons synchronized the zouk guitar chanks. The scene's followers, dubbed Les Doux-Guerriers (The Gentle Warriors), congregate at midnight around dead highway bypasses clad in high-visibility safety vests sewn from plush pastel fleece, oversized knit balaclavas, and fingerless cycling gloves adorned with crushed-cassette jewelry. Their signature ritual involves pouring warm sweetened condensed milk over the hot heat-sinks of hijacked amplifier rigs while unison-singing soaring, operatic high notes in Martinican Creole, turning moments of violent urban gridlock into drowsy, defiant open-air living rooms.

Typical Instruments
- Primary Melodic Voice: Chromatic Rosewood Xylophone (amplified via tape-loop delays and vintage spring tanks)
- High-Velocity Lead: 7-String ESP/Ibanez electric guitars with scalloped fretboards (executed with sweeping neo-classical arpeggios, softened through low-pass filters)
- Rhythmic Foundation: Roland TR-707 and SP-404 samplers playing zouk ti-bwa rimshots, shakers, and softened lo-fi kick-drum triplets
- Harmonic Bed: Fender Rhodes Mark I run through warbling chorus pedals, dusty tape decks, and vintage mini-synthesizers (Yamaha CS-01)
- Atmospheric Layering: Piezo-rigged traffic signal relays, magnetic induction coils capturing overhead powerlines, and binaural rainfall field recordings

